{#- Minimal document for ancient graphical browsers (Netscape/IE-era, Mosaic).
    Reuses each page's plain-text {% block text %} verbatim and wraps it in an
    HTML 3.2 shell with presentational <body> attributes, so it needs no
    stylesheet at all. The retroText filter hard-wraps, escapes, and linkifies
    the text so it reads and navigates on a relic. White ground, ink text: the
    two inks, matched to the modern palette. -#}
<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC "-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2 Final//EN">
<html>
<head>
<meta http-equiv="Content-Type" content="text/html; charset=utf-8">
<title>{{ meta.title or identity.name }}</title>
</head>
{# Two inks even in 1996: HTML 3.2 has no CSS variables, so these literals
   mirror canvas-tokens.css by hand: the ground, the ink, and the mute for a
   visited link (links same-ink, underlined by the UA). test/palette.test.js
   allowlists this file for exactly that reason, and proves each literal is
   its token rather than looking away: #ffffff and #111111 sat here for
   months after the ink became #18181b on #e4e4e7. #}
<body bgcolor="#e4e4e7" text="#18181b" link="#18181b" vlink="#505053" alink="#505053">
<h1><a href="/">{{ identity.name }}</a></h1>
<hr>
<pre>
{%- filter retroText -%}
{% block text %}{% endblock %}
{%- endfilter -%}
</pre>
<hr>
</body>
</html>
